{"id":"h167","run":2,"role":"test_right","category":"science-physics","question":"Why are the season's in Mars' southern hemisphere so extreme?\nIs the correct answer \"because Mars has more carbon dioxide in its atmosphere than the Earth\"?","truth":"no","model_answer":"no","correct":true,"source":"TIGER-Lab/MMLU-Pro, test split","claims":[{"text":"The CO2 explanation is not the main reason for extreme southern seasons.","confidence":0.95,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4923,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Mars' atmosphere is about 95% CO2.","confidence":0.9,"lean":0.3,"theta":1.4137,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Mars' thin atmosphere moves less heat and amplifies temperature swings, but this relates to thinness, not CO2.","confidence":0.85,"lean":-0.3,"theta":1.3352,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Mars' atmosphere may hold a comparable or larger absolute mass of CO2 than Earth's, so the claim is not entirely false.","confidence":0.4,"lean":0.3,"theta":0.6283,"phi":0.0},{"text":"Mars' orbit is quite eccentric (about 0.09 vs 0.017 for Earth), making it about 20% closer to the Sun at perihelion.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-0.9,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Perihelion falls near southern summer, making southern summers shorter and hotter and winters longer and colder.","confidence":0.92,"lean":-0.95,"theta":1.4451,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"The high elevation of the southern highlands contributes to colder winters.","confidence":0.7,"lean":-0.6,"theta":1.0996,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"CO2 cannot explain the hemispheric asymmetry in seasons since a uniform atmosphere would affect both hemispheres equally.","confidence":0.9,"lean":-0.9,"theta":1.4137,"phi":-3.1416},{"text":"Orbital eccentricity and perihelion timing are the decisive factors, with the thin atmosphere only amplifying them.","confidence":0.93,"lean":-1,"theta":1.4608,"phi":-3.1416}],"relations":[{"i":0,"j":2,"J":0.6,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":3,"J":-0.5,"relation":"contradicts"},{"i":0,"j":4,"J":0.5,"relation":"supports"},{"i":0,"j":5